{
  "about": "The bot roster (feature F9): one human-like opponent per 100 Elo from 600 to 2000, then every 200 up to 2600. Each bot is Maia-3 asked to play like a person of that rating. Ratings are on Maia's own scale (Lichess). The band notes describe typical play at that level; they are a guide, not a measurement.",
  "bands": {
    "Beginner": "Develops a little, leaves pieces undefended and misses one-move threats.",
    "Improver": "Knows the opening principles and the simple tactics; still blunders under pressure.",
    "Club player": "Sound openings and two-move tactics; takes what you leave loose.",
    "Strong club player": "Few free gifts. You have to create your own chances.",
    "Expert": "Rarely gives anything away. For when you want to see how strong play looks.",
    "Engine-style": "Stockfish held back to a short search: computer chess, not human chess. Its mistakes are not the ones people make, so it teaches less than the human-like bots; it is here for variety."
  },
